These are build instructions for a system running Windows 11 x64 and Visual Studio 17 2022.
Some libraries may build out of box on alternative setups, but HyperNEAT won't.

It might be best to make a copy of the HyperNEAT file outside of your GitHub folder to mess around with, but the .gitignore does
remove all unwanted build, out, and stage folders, meaning you can build the version in your repo too.

My copy is hosted at "C:\HyperNEAT".




Before you start:

Download:

	CMake GUI
	https://github.com/Kitware/CMake/releases/download/v3.30.5/cmake-3.30.5-windows-x86_64.msi
	
	Python313
	(Make sure you do custom installation and only install 3.13, NOT 3.12.  Installing both will introduce conflicts.)
	https://www.python.org/ftp/python/3.13.0/python-3.13.0-amd64.exe

	


Open the environment variables editor window:

	Create a new system variable:
		PYTHON
		The path to your python 13.3 file.  Mine is just:
	
		C:\Python313

	Edit the Path system variable:
	Add a new entry with the path to the wxWidgets library file.  Mine is:
	
		C:\HyperNEAT\wxWidgets-3.2.6\lib\vc14x_x64_dll

You're ready to start building.




1. Build the boost_1_86_0 library (this is the only build that will not use CMake)

        i. Open Visual Studio and click "continue without code"
       ii. Go to Tools -> Command Line -> Developer Command Prompt
      iii. Navigate to the boost root directory (on my computer it's "C:\HyperNEAT\boost_1_86_0")
              - i.e. enter "cd C:\HyperNEAT_built\boost_1_86_0"
       iv. Type "bootstrap", press enter.
        v. Type ".\b2", press enter.
       vi. This will take a few minutes.
      vii. You're done building boost!

2. Build the Board library

        i. Open the CMake GUI
       ii. Click browse source and select the Board root directory (i.e. "C:\HyperNEAT\Board")
      iii. Click browse build, create a build folder, and select it (i.e. "C:\HyperNEAT\Board\build")
       iv. Click Configure
        v. Under "specify the generator" choose Visual Studio 17 2022.  Select Use default native compilers.
       vi. Click Finish.
      vii. The entries should be red.  Click Configure again.  They should no longer be red.
     viii. Click Generate.
       ix. Click Open Project.  It should open Visual Studio.
        x. Select the Debug build with x64 configuration and build ALL_BUILD
       xi. Select the Release build with x64 configuration and build ALL_BUILD
      xii. You're done building Board!

3. Build the JGTL library

        i. Repeat steps i-xi from 2 for "C:\HyperNEAT\JGTL".
       ii. You're done building JGTL!

4. Build the zlib library

        i. Repeat steps i-xi from 2 for "C:\HyperNEAT\zlib".
       ii. You're done building zlib!

5. Build the tinyxml library

        i. Repeat steps i-xi from 2 for "C:\HyperNEAT\tinyxmldll".
       ii. You're done building tinyxmldll!

6. Build the fuego library

        i. Repeat steps i-xi from 2 for "C:\HyperNEAT\fuego-0.4". (note: the build will take a while)
       ii. You're done building fuego!

7. Build HyperNEAT

        i. Repeat steps i-xi from 2 for "C:\HyperNEAT\NE\HyperNEAT".
       ii. You're done building HyperNEAT!

8. Run HyperNEAT:

        i. In Visual Studio, in the Solution Explorer panel, right click the Hypercube_NEAT entry.
       ii. Click "Set as Startup Project".
      iii. Click "Start Without Debugging" (the play button) or press Ctrl+F5
       iv. The HyperNEAT GUI should now pop up!

For instructions on running experiments in HyperNEAT, take a look at the manual ("C:\HyperNEAT\doc\manual.pdf")


If you have any problems, I'll try to help out!

Happy building!
Megan Krajewski.

